{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_12_1034144510#11_2036760756","relevance":0,"reason":"This is a supplement article about phosphatidylserine and cortisol, with only incidental overlap with stress and memory; it does not answer what studies show about the stress-memory relationship.","learned":"Queries including cortisol and enhancement can drift into supplement marketing rather than stress-memory evidence."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"acute stress\" memory review effects)","reason":"Search for scientific review documents specifically about effects of acute stress on memory, which should directly answer the information need."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"acute stress\" memory review effects)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_36044806#3_82250459","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1202","rank":1,"snippet":"in Mathematics Instruction for Adolescents with Mild Intellectual Disability Educated in Inclusive Classrooms Education of Infectious Diseases Fellows During the COVID-19 Pandemic Crisis: Challenges and Opportunities Stepping up Social–Emotional Learning to Reignite All Brains \" , \"segment\": \"3 Therefore, a large number of studies has been conducted to better understand how stress affects learning and memory. The effects of stress were found to be complex, though, with stress having both enhancing and impairing <cover>effects on memory, depending on the specific memory process or stage that is affected by stress 1, 4 and the activity profile of major physiological stress response systems. This review summarises the current state of knowledge on the impact of (acute) stress </cover>on memory and derives implications for educational settings from these laboratory findings. Because our focus is on memory processes most relevant in the classroom, we will concentrate mainly on the effects of (moderate) stress (induced in laboratory settings) on episodic and semantic memory, as well as the engagement of multiple memory systems in healthy humans (for reviews on the influence of stress on other forms of memory or other cognitive processes, see e.g. Arnsten 5 and Sandi 6 ). "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"3/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_36044806#3_82250459","relevance":3,"reason":"This is a review explicitly dedicated to the impact of acute stress on memory, stating that many studies find complex effects: stress can enhance or impair memory depending on the memory stage/process and physiological stress systems.","learned":"Acute stress has both enhancing and impairing effects on memory, depending on which memory process or stage is affected and the physiological stress-response profile."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1202","reason":"The current query found a directly relevant review; the next ranked results are likely to include more grade-3 evidence on acute stress and memory."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1202"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_36044806#2_82247150","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1202","rank":2,"snippet":"2 which are at the heart of our educational system. Beyond their relevance in educational contexts, stress-induced alterations in learning and memory are also thought to contribute to stress-related mental disorders, such as major depressive disorder or post-traumatic stress disorder. 3 Therefore, a large number of studies has been conducted to better understand how stress affects learning and memory. The effects of stress were found to be complex, though, with stress having both enhancing and impairing <cover>effects on memory, depending on the specific memory process or stage that is affected by stress 1, 4 and the activity profile of major physiological stress response systems. This review summarises the current state of knowledge on the impact of (acute) stress </cover>on memory and derives implications for educational settings from these laboratory findings. Because our focus is on memory processes most relevant in the classroom, we will concentrate mainly on the effects of (moderate) stress (induced in laboratory settings) on episodic and semantic memory, as well as the engagement of multiple memory systems in healthy humans (for reviews on the influence of stress on other forms of memory or other cognitive processes, see e.g. Arnsten 5 and Sandi 6 ). "}}
