{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_04_1249563007#9_2566590557","relevance":2,"reason":"The snippet directly claims excess cortisol can damage short-term memory and that long-term mild stress can lead to broader recall problems, but it appears to be a general anti-stress/wellness article rather than a studies-focused or dedicated evidence document.","learned":"Another source repeats the claim that excess cortisol is associated with short-term memory problems, especially under prolonged stress."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"stress and memory\" encoding retrieval cortisol)","reason":"Search for dedicated stress-and-memory material combining mechanisms and memory stages, aiming to find direct evidence rather than general wellness claims."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"stress and memory\" encoding retrieval cortisol)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_1569600860#4_3202839727","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1226","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_1569600860#4_3202839727\" , \"end_char\": 4278.000000 , \"headings\": \"How Stress Works With and Against Your Memory How Stress Works With and Against Your Memory<cover>Stress and Memory Improve Your Memory Under Stress \" , \"segment\": \"The good news is that there was a short delay between encoding and the formation of memory. Also, if the material being learned was directly related to the stressor, memory actually improved. Even better, post-encoding stress actually improved memory formation and retrieval as well, meaning stress that occurred after the memory was formed actually led to better memory-making. 2 ﻿ Stress increased cortisol, </cover>but the amount of cortisol was not directly related to the effects of stress on memory. This means that if you create more cortisol during your stress response, this won't necessarily mean that your memory will be more impaired than someone who is less hormonally-responsive. Interestingly, women who were on oral contraceptives experienced less of a negative effect. 2 ﻿ Stress can also lead to exhaustion, and this can lead to cognitive impairment "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"33/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_1569600860#4_3202839727","relevance":3,"reason":"This article is dedicated to how stress works with and against memory and directly summarizes study findings: stressor-related material may be remembered better, post-encoding stress can improve memory formation and retrieval, stress increases cortisol, but cortisol amount is not necessarily directly proportional to memory impairment.","learned":"Stress can enhance memory when the learned material is related to the stressor or when stress occurs after encoding; cortisol rises during stress, but higher cortisol response does not always mean worse memory."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1226","reason":"This query is retrieving direct stress-and-memory findings from a dedicated article; continue to collect additional evidence-bearing segments."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1226"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_1569600860#3_3202837750","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1226","rank":2,"snippet":"is easier to recall. This is why false memories can be created with well-intentioned lines of questioning. 1 ﻿ A recent meta-analysis was conducted on 113 stress studies, meaning that researchers examined that many independent studies on <cover>stress and memory to determine what the major findings were. There is ample evidence that stress affects memory, and these studies just lent more support for that research: 2 ﻿ One of the most interesting findings was that stress could impede the formation of memories if it occurred prior to or during encoding, the time during which the memory is formed. The good news is that there was a short delay between encoding and the formation of memory. Also, if the material being learned was directly related to the stressor, memory actually improved. Even better, post-encoding stress actually improved memory formation and retrieval as well, meaning stress that occurred after the memory was formed actually led to better memory-making. 2 ﻿ Stress increased cortisol, </cover>but the amount of cortisol was not directly related to the effects of stress on memory. This means that if you create more cortisol during your stress response, this won't necessarily mean that your memory will be "}}
