{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"stress\" \"memory\" \"long-term memory\" corticosterone)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_16_3299501977#4_3644834446","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1229","rank":1,"snippet":"\"Corticosterone has further developmental effects on birds. Increased levels of corticosterone in chicks leads to increased begging for food and aggressiveness. In the short term this leads to higher chance of obtaining food, but in the long term, increased corticosterone in early life compromises the birds cognitive functioning ( problem solving, association of visual cue with food, etc.). Parental response to increased begging by chicks is an increased time foraging for food. This leaves the nest of chicks without protection for increased durations of time. To counter this, during extended periods of food shortage, chicks of some species may suppress corticosterone activity and thus reduce the negative effects elevated corticosterone induces. Effect on memory Corticosterone has multiple effects on memory. The main effects are seen through the impact of <cover>stress on emotional memories as well as long term memory (LTM). With emotional memories, corticosterone </cover>is largely associated with fear memory recognition. Studies have shown that when fear memories are reactivated or consolidated, levels of corticosterone increased.\" , \"start_char\": 3987.000000 , \"title\": \"Corticosterone - Wikipedia\" , \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corticosterone\" }"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"36/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_16_3299501977#4_3644834446","relevance":3,"reason":"This corticosterone article includes a dedicated 'Effect on memory' section and directly states that corticosterone/stress affects emotional memories and long-term memory, with fear-memory reactivation or consolidation associated with increased corticosterone levels.","learned":"Stress hormones such as corticosterone are linked to emotional memory and long-term memory processes, especially fear-memory recognition, reactivation, and consolidation."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1229","reason":"The query is retrieving direct evidence on stress hormones and long-term memory; continue to find more relevant stress-memory findings from this focused direction."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1229"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_16_3299501977#5_3644835983","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1229","rank":2,"snippet":"3299501977#5_3644835983\" , \"end_char\": 5596.000000 , \"headings\": \"Corticosterone Corticosterone Contents Roles Release or generation mechanisms Corticosterone in birds Effect on memory Additional images See also References External links \" , \"segment\": \"To counter this, during extended periods of food shortage, chicks of some species may suppress corticosterone activity and thus reduce the negative effects elevated corticosterone induces. Effect on memory Corticosterone has multiple effects on memory. The main effects are seen through the impact of <cover>stress on emotional memories as well as long term memory (LTM). With emotional memories, corticosterone </cover>is largely associated with fear memory recognition. Studies have shown that when fear memories are reactivated or consolidated, levels of corticosterone increased. The increase in corticosterone is linked to anxiety relief. This finding depends on the time at which the administration of corticosterone took place as compared to when the fear conditioning took place; corticosterone can either facilitate or interrupt conditioned fear. Not only does corticosterone have effects on emotional memories but memory recognition and consolidation as well. With respect to recognition and long term memories, corticosterone has variable effects.\" , \""}}
{"request":{"judgments":"37/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_16_3299501977#5_3644835983","relevance":3,"reason":"This corticosterone document has a dedicated memory-effects section and directly summarizes stress-hormone findings: corticosterone affects emotional and long-term memory, rises during fear-memory reactivation/consolidation, can facilitate or interrupt conditioned fear depending on timing, and has variable effects on recognition and consolidation.","learned":"Stress hormones can both facilitate and disrupt memory depending on timing and memory type; corticosterone is especially tied to emotional/fear memory, recognition, consolidation, and long-term memory."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1229","reason":"The corticosterone/long-term-memory query is still producing direct evidence on stress hormones and memory, so continue to the next ranked result."}}
